#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110395 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3162bc9-a06e-50d9-aaab-628438daa9fe" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Property of a Bisection"}] :question/id #uuid "9612e5aa-af83-5ea5-8bf2-302834e1926a" :question/math-academy-id "e-2615" :question/problem "In the figure below, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AC}$ is the bisector of $\\angle DAB$ and $m\\angle DAB = 79^\\circ.$ What is the measure of $\\angle DAC?$\n\n![](images/8d/8d02999cf6ae7ec69fee2cdac99b8dc214c59f1ed50bbf85bec6bec01f2212ef.png)" :question/worked-solution "An angle bisector divides the angle into two congruent angles with equal measures, so $m\\angle BAC = m\\angle DAC .$\n\n![](images/6e/6e7c8ce2713441e633d7ecc3548c504d2522b4771eb6355217d6ab766a647b67.png)\n\nEach of these congruent angles has a measure that is half the measure of the larger bisected angle. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DAC & =\\frac{m∠DAB}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{79}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={39.5}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110397 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2051959e-cd96-5318-8b36-518756991988" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Angle Given an Angle Bisector, an Angle Measurement, and a Straight Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "ec3e6d87-e38e-5aa6-b064-f3c15a51a836" :question/math-academy-id "e-2621" :question/problem "In the figure below, ![](images/64/6450e078b6f1dec407838cfd67a8ec79507bc9ffa00ab12fc167d6087dc8cfe4.svg) is the bisector of ![](images/f5/f5f6b946618b35ae916b01cb82e1a5e741f41993f303f02b5c119351d7452346.svg) and ![](images/cf/cf8d89fc393869edb95a2fdb8b75d24bb04f3de676fd73cdd9d8a19af092a00a.svg) What is the measure of ![](images/8d/8d4751af85bf1737162fafc15e01e261b992f958ee7be84e5b03a2c7dcfedfbf.svg)\n\n![](images/91/910939d11fc9c0acdb5fbb7e073b67c8fcbf91ea5a88e74851f0f5f900f5123a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since the angles ![](images/f5/f5f6b946618b35ae916b01cb82e1a5e741f41993f303f02b5c119351d7452346.svg) and ![](images/d0/d0d040e66003036f8fa31da39da08d78506511f501b1a3f5cd1f39efe16f910c.svg) are supplementary, we have \n\n![](images/47/47f63dede9eaaa0f1ed64bc2cc6110bdbab7f3d52052201284417d1bf7336e5a.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/64/6450e078b6f1dec407838cfd67a8ec79507bc9ffa00ab12fc167d6087dc8cfe4.svg) is the bisector of ![](images/f5/f5f6b946618b35ae916b01cb82e1a5e741f41993f303f02b5c119351d7452346.svg), we obtain\n\n![](images/4b/4b9eaaf39d74d934992b68c439bff0bed5c7ef5fa1f0d59620993caed61f0860.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110398 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6849b30-c5a6-53ba-8517-92c9526ce308" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Angle Given an Angle Bisector and Two Angle Measurements"}] :question/id #uuid "f4304d42-1b4c-5ade-86d2-8ed44a952fc8" :question/math-academy-id "e-2624" :question/problem "In the figure below, $\\overrightarrow{OC}$ is the bisector of $\\angle AOB.$ If $m \\angle AOD = 90^\\circ$ and $m \\angle COD = 35^\\circ,$ find the measure of $\\angle BOD.$\n\n![](images/0b/0b76c6ee6e576fa2701993e941c27f1168c213d9f5f26b1aa8a50759c3711404.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/5a/5ae4873418247e8d825233f903ed359ebee4d2c51ad73e6c7763102a23acded6.png)\n\nBy the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC & =m∠AOD-m∠COD \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}-{35}^{∘} \\\\  & ={55}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm \\angle AOB =2 m \\angle AOC = 110^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFinally, the angle addition postulate gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOD & =m∠AOB-m∠AOD \\\\  & ={110}^{∘}-{90}^{∘} \\\\  & ={20}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110399 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110770 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6720ccef-3a50-59f0-a1e3-6158ef29ab4e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Angle Given Two Angle Bisectors"}] :question/id #uuid "b3dbb627-0262-5fdd-ae6b-ba669e4fac6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-2628" :question/problem "In the figure below, $\\angle BAD$ is a straight angle, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AG}$ is the bisector of $\\angle CAD$ and $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AF}$ is the bisector of $\\angle BAC .$ What is the measure of $\\angle GAF?$\n\n![](images/45/458893f38204421326fd7dcb0693b1688eeb61fb3988e00b2d36763b47adc2d2.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/3b/3b0fddd3229947725b0f4f07e58ce9bfac3a9e7e348c25757162f5bc8e03d699.png)\n\nLet $m \\angle FAC=x$ and $m \\angle CAG=y.$ Then,\n\n$$\nm \\angle GAF=m\\angle FAC + m \\angle CAG=x+y.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AF}$ is the bisector of $\\angle BAC$, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle BAC=2x.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AG}$ is the bisector of $\\angle CAD$, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle CAD=2y.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\angle BAD$ is a straight angle, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BAC+m∠CAD & =m∠BAD \\\\ 2x+2y & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x+y & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $m \\angle GAF=x+y=90^\\circ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3162bc9-a06e-50d9-aaab-628438daa9fe" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Property of a Bisection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b2bf445-6884-5938-84f2-92ebe3b004cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507730} {:answer/id #uuid "5da70166-d661-5d68-9b37-57eb151b9a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{13}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507736} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdee111-db70-58d2-9682-ad26b784e60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{9}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507777} {:answer/id #uuid "90353fa5-7f89-514b-ab76-2eb5bc33c3ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{15}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507803} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b820d6-1e83-5f7b-9a5d-7b829738275d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bdee111-db70-58d2-9682-ad26b784e60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{9}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507777} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e88a87f-5bc0-5820-a8c2-8fb21a9941e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b7d3007-c733-562f-b061-3d6372ea77d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-117143" :question/problem "![](images/62/6239a47a2bd88b631f9058c7afe3b59c10b8e71fb8957fe7a354cc7e72daf69d.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $m∠BOC={72}^{∘}$ and $m∠AOC=7x+{9}^{∘}.$ If $\\overset{\\to }{OC}$ is the bisector of $∠AOB,$ find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/6a/6a49c1d69a34d0159cd24869b51cb7a517021a147e0e831b969c1135f6b533fc.png)\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Thus, $∠AOC≅∠BOC.$ Therefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC & =m∠BOC \\\\ 7x+{9}^{∘} & ={72}^{∘} \\\\ 7x & ={63}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={9}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3162bc9-a06e-50d9-aaab-628438daa9fe" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Property of a Bisection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095a92cd-dd47-564d-9d40-64701fd71e1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507611} {:answer/id #uuid "0bbda6e3-12f9-5be3-805d-19c2abbdc436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{27}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507614} {:answer/id #uuid "731fc0c1-49c0-5337-8789-e76a292ba140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{24}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507766} {:answer/id #uuid "94502480-4b15-54ca-9900-12ac33fe1398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{29}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507808} {:answer/id #uuid "eca63fd7-5377-556e-9fe0-f34f11da1ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94502480-4b15-54ca-9900-12ac33fe1398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{29}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507808} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0bd8a51-6747-5bfc-b459-183d1248b264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66ea6fa9-fa1e-5e19-8e8b-006a3addc0db" :question/math-academy-id "q-117146" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f818ebe4aea90c33ba1eb3feccf988b0486565da9177df1e98460a950f8b22e.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overset{\\to }{OC}$ is the bisector of $∠AOB.$ If $m∠AOB={58}^{∘}$, find $m∠AOC.$" :question/worked-solution "The bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Each of these congruent angles has a measure that is half the measure of the larger bisected angle. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC & =\\frac{m∠AOB}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{58}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={29}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6849b30-c5a6-53ba-8517-92c9526ce308" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Angle Given an Angle Bisector and Two Angle Measurements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c6f6718-abd0-54bc-8577-dd9269708b63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507689} {:answer/id #uuid "6611433c-bc3c-5849-8340-2ff3e742bd49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{38}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507748} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd8615d-c2fa-5d36-8505-c05c264107fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{52}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507820} {:answer/id #uuid "df5e8c07-4967-569a-a41e-92fd0ecb376a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{26}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507899} {:answer/id #uuid "f691a25a-c9d1-5a98-b17a-b5e5771466a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{23}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f691a25a-c9d1-5a98-b17a-b5e5771466a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{23}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507930} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5434d2b-7760-524c-b9cd-5f9df1cefcab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e88bb8a0-0e79-51e0-a5d2-1fbecece5fbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-117261" :question/problem "![](images/23/23794678967f5b2cd1d409f116a9083aa60f942737ad41fa960e91263691f8c7.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ is the bisector of $∠COA.$ If $m∠BOA={127}^{∘}$ and $m∠BOD={75}^{∘},$ find $m∠BOC.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/72/72c0047a8d0b513a5159d31addf0e4c0c2eeefe0e73ceeadf8e154c37f583039.png)\n\nBy the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DOA & =m∠BOA-m∠BOD \\\\  & ={127}^{∘}-{75}^{∘} \\\\  & ={52}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠DOA=m∠COD={52}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOC & =m∠BOD-m∠COD \\\\  & ={75}^{∘}-{52}^{∘} \\\\  & ={23}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2051959e-cd96-5318-8b36-518756991988" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Angle Given an Angle Bisector, an Angle Measurement, and a Straight Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e17997f-a493-55a9-a1fe-8d56e06efc3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e17997f-a493-55a9-a1fe-8d56e06efc3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507631} :answer-field/id #uuid "73740857-daba-5806-a042-08e1179b150d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a317f88-19a8-56a2-bc2b-47dd254aaf3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-173763" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0662ae0e6957786ab7f3821a932e1f36a1c8ca6e8299709daa5e323601194fa.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠BOA$ is a straight angle, and $\\overset{\\to }{OC}$ is the bisector of $∠BOD.$ If $m∠DOA={84}^{∘},$ then $m∠COA=${{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/52/5291d267ce1e895cfe75261a36c9dbc660a32935b6858ec8986379ee8c8bb766.png)\n\nSince the angles $∠BOD$ and $∠DOA$ are supplementary, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOD & ={180}^{∘}-m∠DOA \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{84}^{∘} \\\\  & ={96}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COD & =\\frac{m∠BOD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{96}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={48}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COA & =m∠COD+m∠DOA \\\\  & ={48}^{∘}+{84}^{∘} \\\\  & ={132}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110770 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6720ccef-3a50-59f0-a1e3-6158ef29ab4e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Angle Given Two Angle Bisectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c24ee1b-7520-5096-ab22-3ef897d113ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{116}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507687} {:answer/id #uuid "44d3c912-8452-5c6a-a7e7-92d51c858804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507703} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb29a8b-7700-5acc-8d15-bd00df2cc0b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{72}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507780} {:answer/id #uuid "c27985f4-8af3-51bf-808c-b77637a70153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{86}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507869} {:answer/id #uuid "ca83119f-a6ce-53eb-98c9-eb804eeeb9b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{104}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44d3c912-8452-5c6a-a7e7-92d51c858804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507703} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e8f30c6-2b3d-5a8b-bcbe-f22b8602f636" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cee4d62e-6294-5674-b439-787997c480d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-52777" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7bd5be67491f109f8041d33768aca872f9dc7242dd1d91bcbfe116a8277f5bf1.png)\n\nIn the diagram above $∠BOA$ is a straight angle. Given that $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{OE}$ are the bisectors of $∠COA$ and $∠BOC$, respectively, find $m∠EOD.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/19/19334b594e8567464103bacc4f0978cbbbc664c3cc5a1a6edd5789e822030c4b.png)\n\nLet $m∠COD=x$ and $m∠EOC=y.$ Then,\n\n$$\nm∠EOD=m∠COD+m∠EOC=x+y.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{⟶}{OD}$ is a bisector of $∠COA,$ we have\n\n$$\nm∠COA=2x.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{⟶}{OE}$ is a bisector of $∠BOC,$ we have\n\n$$\nm∠BOC=2y.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $∠AOB$ is a straight angle, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COA+m∠BOC & =m∠AOB \\\\ 2x+2y & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x+y & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $m∠EOD=x+y={90}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2051959e-cd96-5318-8b36-518756991988" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Angle Given an Angle Bisector, an Angle Measurement, and a Straight Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ed48092-8b56-5958-b002-24af91be87f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{144}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507618} {:answer/id #uuid "33749aa2-09af-5c2d-b96b-976090bb1b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{153}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507670} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0e0449-c2d9-5e5a-938e-2b2dedda00ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{154}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507732} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce5961e-51ea-502c-a3cb-8e87aec4d386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{157}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507755} {:answer/id #uuid "c19dfe82-0c5d-53a9-8feb-9d68319f4e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{147}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ce5961e-51ea-502c-a3cb-8e87aec4d386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{157}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507755} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ff6924a-93f5-5682-9c89-7cd766ada809" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c102a149-79c1-57ef-8166-900c7a1db3c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-76634" :question/problem "![](images/38/38f49977e7d2f280dec7b433e167b3e081ed10716715e7c9caa669e57fe0701a.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠BOA$ is a straight angle,  and $\\overset{\\to }{OC}$ is the bisector of $∠BOD.$ If $m∠DOA={134}^{∘},$ find $m∠COA.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/8d/8dfbfa4aaa55e2f8175fe829fccc945d29733a84fc5c1641f099b09d805ada2b.png)\n\nSince the angles $∠BOD$ and $∠DOA$ are supplementary, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOD & ={180}^{∘}-m∠DOA \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{134}^{∘} \\\\  & ={46}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COD & =\\frac{m∠BOD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{46}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={23}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COA & =m∠COD+m∠DOA \\\\  & ={23}^{∘}+{134}^{∘} \\\\  & ={157}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6849b30-c5a6-53ba-8517-92c9526ce308" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Angle Given an Angle Bisector and Two Angle Measurements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5157657c-e339-58bf-a4e5-8e82ad853592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507720} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf873ed-3ddb-528f-bd21-acec47a39882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{29}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507779} {:answer/id #uuid "f33e3e77-56b9-5061-8045-35c0c44ba3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{27}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507925} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bbdf67-2306-56d3-bb7a-706ea3e24690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507926} {:answer/id #uuid "f907ed05-43a0-59a4-b9b8-3c6e5e8236a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{32}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bf873ed-3ddb-528f-bd21-acec47a39882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{29}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507779} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd564ea-42d3-5ae4-839f-5f3370875152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c39734e-60e8-53eb-a61a-e2f6712a7e91" :question/math-academy-id "q-76640" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7ba1daa74999732ba112150ad8ecb0958f80057a4aeb8ff52c96bf9b44a8337c.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ is the bisector of $∠AOC.$ If $m∠BOA={131}^{∘}$ and $m∠BOC={73}^{∘},$ find $m∠DOA.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/68/68c84f70ff4fcd384c21414958ea696469ac6c64749d31c7db7f8792599d77ce.png)\n\nBy the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COA & =m∠BOA-m∠BOC \\\\  & ={131}^{∘}-{73}^{∘} \\\\  & ={58}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DOA & =\\frac{m∠COA}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{58}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={29}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110770 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6720ccef-3a50-59f0-a1e3-6158ef29ab4e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Angle Given Two Angle Bisectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "224af101-a2f4-5dd9-a26c-78344c7d7253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507638} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb2d579-1bb9-570b-b005-855502153d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{65}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507758} {:answer/id #uuid "75b52ce4-32d0-5e14-acc3-8d7dcec57c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{91}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507770} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a926d8-2779-5f8e-b1ec-351f4137e51e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{88}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507826} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e5316c-1c70-55a9-82c5-9dee5b4c206e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1a926d8-2779-5f8e-b1ec-351f4137e51e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{88}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507826} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cf32679-392e-572a-a09d-34cd048592df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b176a2ae-2130-5324-9a8b-d961a2933f07" :question/math-academy-id "q-76644" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb5fddbf843a89ffbefeaed1976a2bfd159fea9a41665f99aee260f110016e1c.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{OE}$ are the bisectors of $∠AOC$ and $∠BOC,$ respectively. If $m∠DOC=x,$ $m∠COE=2x+{2}^{∘},$ and $m∠AOB={130}^{∘},$ find the value of $m∠BOC.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/48/484a70ad0c9eeca1ea03db65f5c687074189ac93f9894b64598278dc285d0d83.png)\n\nFirst, let's express the measures of $∠AOC$ and $∠BOC,$ in terms of $x.$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ is the bisector of $∠AOC,$ we have\n\n$$\nm∠AOC=2m∠DOC=2x.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\to }{OE}$ is the bisector of $∠BOC,$ we have\n\n$$\nm∠BOC=2m∠COE=4x+{4}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSince $m∠AOB={130}^{∘},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC+m∠BOC & =m∠AOB \\\\ 2x+4x+{4}^{∘} & ={130}^{∘} \\\\ 6x+{4}^{∘} & ={130}^{∘} \\\\ 6x & ={126}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={21}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are asked to find $m∠BOC.$ Substituting $x={21}^{∘}$ into the expression for $m∠BOC$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOC & =4({21}^{∘})+{4}^{∘} \\\\  & ={84}^{∘}+{4}^{∘} \\\\  & ={88}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
